-    <p class="section-sub">What each AEGIS stage actually does, mapped to the real Guidewire touchpoint it depends on.</p>
-
-    <details class="spec" id="spec-ingestion" open>
-        <summary>
-            <span class="spec-tag">01</span>
-            <span class="spec-name">Decision Ingestion</span>
-            <span class="spec-status live">LIVE</span>
-        </summary>
-        <div class="spec-body">
-            <table>
-                <tr><th>Guidewire touchpoint</th><td>A Qusar agent issues a decision on top of a PolicyCenter (underwriting) or ClaimCenter (claims) transaction — that decision event is what AEGIS ingests.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>Trigger</th><td><code>POST /api/decisions</code> — either a real agent feed calling this endpoint, or (today, no sandbox access) <code>MockDecisionGenerator</code> calling the same code path on a schedule.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>AEGIS action</th><td>Validates required fields, generates a decision ID/timestamp if missing, persists an immutable audit row independent of Guidewire's own logs, then publishes it in-process for detection to react to.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>Why it matters</th><td>You can't build an exposure/coverage evidence trail for a decision that was never recorded outside the agent's own black box. This is the system of record everything else depends on.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>Implementation</th><td>Fully built. <code>MockDecisionGenerator</code> emits decisions in the exact shape a real Qusar feed would use, so swapping in a live feed only touches this one class.</td></tr>
-            </table>
-        </div>
-    </details>
 
-    <details class="spec" id="spec-detection">
-        <summary>
-            <span class="spec-tag">02</span>
-            <span class="spec-name">Detection &amp; Compliance</span>
-            <span class="spec-status live">LIVE</span>
-        </summary>
-        <div class="spec-body">
-            <table>
-                <tr><th>Guidewire touchpoint</th><td>Runs against every decision regardless of which Guidewire product generated it — same rule engine for an underwriting call and a claims call.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>Trigger</th><td>In-process Spring event, fired synchronously the instant ingestion persists a decision — no polling delay.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>AEGIS action</th><td>Two rules: (1) any <code>FRAUD_FLAG</code> decision outcome <code>FLAGGED</code> always routes to review; (2) an agent whose <code>DENIED</code> rate crosses 50% over a 5+ decision sample gets its latest denial flagged as a statistical outlier. A flagged decision is tagged with the disclosure standard its jurisdiction calls for (EU AI Act, UK FCA, IRDAI, or a US state rule) — a tag for a human reviewer to check against, not a certification that the decision satisfies it.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>Why it matters</th><td>Regulators increasingly require human-reviewable explainability for automated decisions. Manual review can't keep pace with agent volume — this catches what needs a second look without slowing the agent down.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>Implementation</th><td>Fully built and rule-tested (4 unit tests covering clean, always-flag, rate-outlier, and below-threshold cases). Rules are intentionally simple, demonstrative logic — not a certified compliance engine.</td></tr>
-            </table>
-        </div>
-    </details>
-
-    <details class="spec" id="spec-evidence">
-        <summary>
-            <span class="spec-tag">03</span>
-            <span class="spec-name">Evidence Mapping</span>
-            <span class="spec-status live">LIVE</span>
-        </summary>
-        <div class="spec-body">
-            <table>
-                <tr><th>Guidewire touchpoint</th><td>Stands downstream of every Guidewire-triggered decision, turning AEGIS's own governance findings into evidence rather than a claims payment or any other automated action.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>Trigger</th><td>Two independent paths: a detection flag creates an evidence record immediately, tagged with the decision's exposure category; a scheduled sweep (every 10s) catches any decision whose compliance review sat unresolved past a 45s review window and records it as a control gap anyway.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>AEGIS action</th><td>Maps the decision type to a broad insurance exposure category (Underwriting Risk, Claims Handling, or Fraud &amp; Integrity Exposure), persists an evidence audit row with the category and reason, and publishes the result.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>Why it matters</th><td>AEGIS doesn't pay a claim or certify anything — it makes sure every AI decision that needed a second look actually produces evidence a human can review, instead of disappearing into the agent's own logs.</td></tr>
-                <tr><th>Implementation</th><td>Fully built, unit-tested, and the review-gap sweep path was verified end-to-end running locally.</td></tr>
-            </table>
-        </div>
-    </details>
-
